<ReportItem port="0" svc_name="general" protocol="tcp" severity="0" pluginID="44657" pluginName="Linux Daemons with Broken Links to Executables" pluginFamily="Misc.">
<agent>unix</agent>
<description>By examining the &apos;/proc&apos; filesystem on the remote Linux host, Nessus has identified at least one currently-running daemon for which the link to the corresponding executable is broken.

This can occur when the executable associated with a daemon is replaced on disk but the daemon itself has not been restarted.  And if the changes are security-related, the system may remain vulnerable to attack until the daemon is restarted.

Alternatively, it could result from an attacker removing files in an effort to hide malicious activity.</description>
<fname>daemons_with_broken_links.nasl</fname>
<plugin_modification_date>2019/08/30</plugin_modification_date>
<plugin_name>Linux Daemons with Broken Links to Executables</plugin_name>
<plugin_publication_date>2010/02/17</plugin_publication_date>
<plugin_type>local</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>None</risk_factor>
<script_version>1.8</script_version>
<solution>Inspect each reported daemon to determine why the link to the executable is broken.</solution>
<synopsis>A daemon on the remote Linux host may need to be restarted.</synopsis>
<plugin_output>
The following daemons are associated with broken links to
executables :

  - 9000 tcp: (/opt/splunkforwarder/splunkforwarder/bin/splunkd)
  - 8089 tcp: (/opt/splunkforwarder/splunkforwarder/bin/splunkd)
</plugin_output>
</ReportItem>
